{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 42, :comments ["<b>And you shall make them linen breeches.</b> It is difficult: why did He postpone the commandment of the breeches? And one may say, because He said ״וְהִלְבַּשְׁתָּ אוֹתָם אֶת אַהֲרֹן״, therefore He postponed the breeches to say that the commandment of ״וְהִלְבַּשְׁתָּ״ etc. does not apply to them. And one should not say that He intended to say that putting them on should be last, for Scripture says elsewhere (Leviticus 16:4) ״וּמִכְנְסֵי בַד יִהְיוּ עַל בְּשָׂרוֹ״, and they interpreted in Yoma (Yoma 23a) that nothing should precede the breeches — but rather, for the reason we have written." "It further appears that He intended to write it at the end and to juxtapose to it the commandment of ״וְהָיוּ עַל אַהֲרֹן״ etc. (Exodus 28:43) ״וְלֹא יִשְׂאוּ עָוֹן וָמֵתוּ״, for had He included it with the rest of the garments and said ״וְהָיוּ עַל אַהֲרֹן״ etc., the reasoning would lead one to say that the warning applies only to the other garments, but the breeches come only so that nakedness not be exposed and they carry no requirement of the sanctity of the avodah. Therefore He stated them at the end and juxtaposed to them the warning and the punishment, to say that it is binding for the sanctity of the priesthood like the other garments."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:34:24.185369634Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}